1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,254
<font color="#FFFFFF">Most visual question answering models are black
boxes: they output an answer,</font>

2
00:00:04,334 --> 00:00:08,088
<font color="#FFFFFF">but not the reasoning that produced it.</font>

3
00:00:08,168 --> 00:00:11,922
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper,</font>

4
00:00:12,002 --> 00:00:15,757
<font color="#FFFFFF">Towards Reasoning-Aware Explainable VQA from the
University of Illinois and Amazon Alexa AI,</font>

5
00:00:15,837 --> 00:00:19,591
<font color="#FFFFFF">augments a state-of-the-art coarse-to-fine VQA
backbone with an end-to-end explanation generation module,</font>

6
00:00:19,671 --> 00:00:23,425
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the model produces a human-readable textual
explanation alongside every answer while</font>

7
00:00:23,505 --> 00:00:27,259
<font color="#FFFFFF">keeping accuracy essentially unchanged.</font>

8
00:00:27,339 --> 00:00:31,093
<font color="#FFFFFF">It compares LSTM and Transformer decoders as the
explanation generator, and,</font>

9
00:00:31,173 --> 00:00:34,928
<font color="#FFFFFF">crucially,</font>

10
00:00:35,008 --> 00:00:38,762
<font color="#FFFFFF">shows through a large human study that the standard
string-matching metrics like BLEU</font>

11
00:00:38,842 --> 00:00:42,596
<font color="#FFFFFF">and ROUGE are unreliable for judging explanations,</font>

12
00:00:42,676 --> 00:00:46,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">motivating better evaluation.</font>

13
00:00:46,810 --> 00:00:49,608
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the classic visual question answering task,</font>

14
00:00:49,688 --> 00:00:53,361
<font color="#FFFFFF">a model takes an image and a question and returns
an answer.</font>

15
00:00:53,441 --> 00:00:57,052
<font color="#FFFFFF">The field has poured enormous effort into raising
accuracy,</font>

16
00:00:57,132 --> 00:01:00,243
<font color="#FFFFFF">thanks to large pre-trained vision-language models,</font>

17
00:01:00,323 --> 00:01:04,935
<font color="#FFFFFF">but almost no attention is paid to how a model
actually reaches its answer.</font>

18
00:01:05,015 --> 00:01:08,375
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result is a black box: the prediction may be right,</font>

19
00:01:08,455 --> 00:01:12,129
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet there is no evidence explaining the reasoning
behind it.</font>

20
00:01:12,209 --> 00:01:17,008
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors distinguish three kinds of models:
those that give only an answer,</font>

21
00:01:17,088 --> 00:01:19,886
<font color="#FFFFFF">those that add a generic caption of the image,</font>

22
00:01:19,966 --> 00:01:25,954
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the rare third kind that produces a logically
self-contained explanation matching the answer.</font>

23
00:01:26,034 --> 00:01:30,583
<font color="#FFFFFF">Almost all state-of-the-art VQA models fall into
the first two categories,</font>

24
00:01:30,663 --> 00:01:32,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">which motivates this work.</font>

25
00:01:32,590 --> 00:01:36,753
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two open questions drive the paper.</font>

26
00:01:36,833 --> 00:01:40,996
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

27
00:01:41,076 --> 00:01:45,239
<font color="#FFFFFF">can a VQA model generate a human-readable
explanation while still maintaining its answer accuracy?</font>

28
00:01:45,319 --> 00:01:49,482
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, how good are those generated explanations,</font>

29
00:01:49,562 --> 00:01:53,725
<font color="#FFFFFF">and how should we even evaluate them?</font>

30
00:01:53,805 --> 00:01:57,968
<font color="#FFFFFF">Existing explainable-VQA datasets suggest using
conventional natural language metrics such as BLEU and ROUGE,</font>

31
00:01:58,048 --> 00:02:02,211
<font color="#FFFFFF">but these were designed for string matching over
overlapping n-grams,</font>

32
00:02:02,291 --> 00:02:06,454
<font color="#FFFFFF">not for judging whether an explanation truly
supports an answer.</font>

33
00:02:06,534 --> 00:02:10,697
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors argue that as reasoning problems in VQA
grow more complex,</font>

34
00:02:10,777 --> 00:02:14,940
<font color="#FFFFFF">having an interpretable, well-evaluated explanation
is no longer optional but urgent.</font>

35
00:02:15,320 --> 00:02:19,442
<font color="#FFFFFF">The contribution is two-fold.</font>

36
00:02:19,522 --> 00:02:23,644
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, the authors present simple,</font>

37
00:02:23,724 --> 00:02:27,847
<font color="#FFFFFF">easy-to-implement methods that sit on top of a
state-of-the-art VQA framework and maintain</font>

38
00:02:27,927 --> 00:02:32,049
<font color="#FFFFFF">VQA accuracy while generating human-readable
textual explanations.</font>

39
00:02:32,129 --> 00:02:36,251
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

40
00:02:36,331 --> 00:02:40,453
<font color="#FFFFFF">they provide both quantitative experimental results
and a large human study of the proposed explainable VQA method.</font>

41
00:02:40,533 --> 00:02:44,656
<font color="#FFFFFF">Together these illustrate the urgency of proposing
new metrics to evaluate predicted explanations</font>

42
00:02:44,736 --> 00:02:48,858
<font color="#FFFFFF">in vision-language reasoning problems like VQA,</font>

43
00:02:48,938 --> 00:02:53,060
<font color="#FFFFFF">since the metrics in common use today do not
reliably reflect explanation quality.</font>

44
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:59,214
<font color="#FFFFFF">The architecture has two major parts:
coarse-to-fine visual-language reasoning for the answer,</font>

45
00:02:59,294 --> 00:03:01,519
<font color="#FFFFFF">and an explanation generation module.</font>

46
00:03:01,599 --> 00:03:07,000
<font color="#FFFFFF">A pre-trained Faster-RCNN extracts
region-of-interest features and image predicates such</font>

47
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:08,930
<font color="#FFFFFF">as object and attribute labels,</font>

48
00:03:09,010 --> 00:03:10,799
<font color="#FFFFFF">which are embedded with GloVe.</font>

49
00:03:10,879 --> 00:03:14,972
<font color="#FFFFFF">The question is encoded with GloVe embeddings
passed through a GRU,</font>

50
00:03:15,052 --> 00:03:19,082
<font color="#FFFFFF">and question predicates are extracted by filtering
out stop words.</font>

51
00:03:19,162 --> 00:03:23,068
<font color="#FFFFFF">These signals flow through three modules:
information filtering,</font>

52
00:03:23,148 --> 00:03:26,805
<font color="#FFFFFF">which removes noisy region information; multimodal
learning,</font>

53
00:03:26,885 --> 00:03:33,158
<font color="#FFFFFF">which uses bilinear attention networks to fuse
image and question at both coarse and fine granularity;</font>

54
00:03:33,238 --> 00:03:37,953
<font color="#FFFFFF">and semantic reasoning, which combines the two
levels into a joint embedding.</font>

55
00:03:38,033 --> 00:03:45,240
<font color="#FFFFFF">That joint embedding is sent both to a multi-layer
perceptron for answer prediction and to the explanation generator.</font>

56
00:03:45,320 --> 00:03:47,732
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two generator architectures are studied,</font>

57
00:03:47,812 --> 00:03:51,157
<font color="#FFFFFF">a two-layer LSTM and an eight-head Transformer decoder,</font>

58
00:03:51,237 --> 00:03:57,447
<font color="#FFFFFF">each trained with autoregressive cross-entropy loss
and teacher forcing on ground-truth explanations.</font>

59
00:03:57,527 --> 00:04:02,990
<font color="#FFFFFF">The whole system is trained end-to-end with a
combined loss that balances the answer term</font>

60
00:04:03,070 --> 00:04:05,980
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the explanation term through a factor alpha.</font>

61
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:10,768
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because very few datasets provide annotated
explanations alongside answers,</font>

62
00:04:10,848 --> 00:04:15,257
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors chose the two largest available.</font>

63
00:04:15,337 --> 00:04:19,745
<font color="#FFFFFF">GQA-REX contains explanations for roughly
ninety-eight percent of the samples in the GQA-balanced dataset,</font>

64
00:04:19,825 --> 00:04:24,233
<font color="#FFFFFF">about 1.04 million question-answer pairs spanning
eighty-two thousand images,</font>

65
00:04:24,313 --> 00:04:28,722
<font color="#FFFFFF">with one explanation per pair.</font>

66
00:04:28,802 --> 00:04:33,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">However,</font>

67
00:04:33,290 --> 00:04:37,698
<font color="#FFFFFF">its explanations follow the reasoning-format of
prior work and are not fully human-readable,</font>

68
00:04:37,778 --> 00:04:42,187
<font color="#FFFFFF">sometimes containing grammatical inaccuracies.</font>

69
00:04:42,267 --> 00:04:46,675
<font color="#FFFFFF">VQA-E provides explanations for about forty percent
of the question-answer pairs in VQA 2.0,</font>

70
00:04:46,755 --> 00:04:51,163
<font color="#FFFFFF">and because those explanations are built by
matching captions to the question-answer pair,</font>

71
00:04:51,243 --> 00:04:55,652
<font color="#FFFFFF">they tend to read more like image captions than
genuine reasoning.</font>

72
00:04:55,732 --> 00:05:00,140
<font color="#FFFFFF">Both datasets have limitations, which the authors
are transparent about.</font>

73
00:05:00,520 --> 00:05:05,864
<font color="#FFFFFF">The central result is that adding explanation
generation does not cost accuracy.</font>

74
00:05:05,944 --> 00:05:11,424
<font color="#FFFFFF">On GQA-REX the model reaches a VQA score of
seventy-seven point four nine percent,</font>

75
00:05:11,504 --> 00:05:14,814
<font color="#FFFFFF">and on VQA-E seventy-one point four eight percent,</font>

76
00:05:14,894 --> 00:05:21,052
<font color="#FFFFFF">both essentially matching the baseline that was
trained without any explanation supervision.</font>

77
00:05:21,132 --> 00:05:25,866
<font color="#FFFFFF">Varying the balance factor alpha changes the
trade-off only marginally.</font>

78
00:05:25,946 --> 00:05:28,035
<font color="#FFFFFF">For the explanations themselves,</font>

79
00:05:28,115 --> 00:05:32,442
<font color="#FFFFFF">the CFRF-plus-LSTM model outperforms the prior
baseline on VQA-E,</font>

80
00:05:32,522 --> 00:05:38,206
<font color="#FFFFFF">with a BLEU-1 of zero point three three versus zero
point two six eight and a ROUGE-L</font>

81
00:05:38,286 --> 00:05:42,342
<font color="#FFFFFF">of zero point three two five versus zero point two
four nine.</font>

82
00:05:42,422 --> 00:05:47,291
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors are candid that these absolute numbers
are only satisfactory,</font>

83
00:05:47,371 --> 00:05:50,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">which sets up their argument about evaluation.</font>

84
00:05:50,790 --> 00:05:55,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors ablate two design choices: the balance
factor alpha that weights answer loss against explanation loss,</font>

85
00:05:55,460 --> 00:06:00,050
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the choice of explanation generator.</font>

86
00:06:00,130 --> 00:06:04,720
<font color="#FFFFFF">On GQA-REX,</font>

87
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:09,390
<font color="#FFFFFF">the LSTM variant ranges from about seventy-five to
seventy-seven and a half percent across different alpha values,</font>

88
00:06:09,470 --> 00:06:14,060
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the Transformer decoder at alpha equal to one
half reaches seventy-seven point zero six percent.</font>

89
00:06:14,140 --> 00:06:18,730
<font color="#FFFFFF">On VQA-E,</font>

90
00:06:18,810 --> 00:06:23,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">every configuration lands within a fraction of a
percent of the seventy-one and a half percent baseline.</font>

91
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:28,070
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway from these sweeps is that answer
accuracy is remarkably stable no matter</font>

92
00:06:28,150 --> 00:06:32,740
<font color="#FFFFFF">how the explanation loss is weighted or which
decoder architecture generates the explanation,</font>

93
00:06:32,820 --> 00:06:37,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the explanation module can be added essentially
for free.</font>

94
00:06:37,790 --> 00:06:40,498
<font color="#FFFFFF">Four numbers summarize the paper's impact.</font>

95
00:06:40,578 --> 00:06:46,805
<font color="#FFFFFF">The model achieves a seventy-seven point four nine
percent VQA score on GQA-REX and seventy-one</font>

96
00:06:46,885 --> 00:06:49,062
<font color="#FFFFFF">point four eight percent on VQA-E,</font>

97
00:06:49,142 --> 00:06:51,120
<font color="#FFFFFF">both at state-of-the-art level.</font>

98
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:52,381
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the human study,</font>

99
00:06:52,461 --> 00:06:58,223
<font color="#FFFFFF">sixty-five point one six percent of the generated
explanations were judged by annotators</font>

100
00:06:58,303 --> 00:07:01,011
<font color="#FFFFFF">to genuinely lead to the predicted answer.</font>

101
00:07:01,091 --> 00:07:04,264
<font color="#FFFFFF">And in roughly sixty point five percent of cases,</font>

102
00:07:04,344 --> 00:07:09,840
<font color="#FFFFFF">the model both predicts the correct answer and
generates a valid explanation for it.</font>

103
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:15,549
<font color="#FFFFFF">The human study itself was substantial: four
thousand seven hundred thirty-five unique</font>

104
00:07:15,629 --> 00:07:18,935
<font color="#FFFFFF">image-question pairs from the VQA-E validation set,</font>

105
00:07:19,015 --> 00:07:20,993
<font color="#FFFFFF">each rated by three annotators,</font>

106
00:07:21,073 --> 00:07:25,640
<font color="#FFFFFF">for over fourteen thousand responses from one
hundred eleven subjects.</font>

107
00:07:26,020 --> 00:07:29,984
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting message is twofold.</font>

108
00:07:30,064 --> 00:07:34,028
<font color="#FFFFFF">Practically,</font>

109
00:07:34,108 --> 00:07:38,072
<font color="#FFFFFF">explanation generation can be added to a
state-of-the-art VQA backbone with almost no loss in answer accuracy,</font>

110
00:07:38,152 --> 00:07:42,116
<font color="#FFFFFF">giving users a human-readable reason alongside each
answer.</font>

111
00:07:42,196 --> 00:07:46,160
<font color="#FFFFFF">But methodologically,</font>

112
00:07:46,240 --> 00:07:50,204
<font color="#FFFFFF">the paper shows through concrete examples that
string-matching metrics like BLEU and ROUGE</font>

113
00:07:50,284 --> 00:07:54,248
<font color="#FFFFFF">can reward a wrong explanation and penalize a valid one,</font>

114
00:07:54,328 --> 00:07:58,292
<font color="#FFFFFF">so they are unreliable for this task.</font>

115
00:07:58,372 --> 00:08:02,336
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors therefore argue that the real
bottleneck for explainable VQA is not the generator but the evaluation,</font>

116
00:08:02,416 --> 00:08:06,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">and they urge the community to develop proper
reasoning-aware metrics for judging explanations.</font>
